            sample: "Strong responses will argue that Alcott gives moral choices real physical consequences. A clear structure might begin with Beth contracts scarlet fever after caring for the Hummels and never fully recovers. It could then connect this to the novel moves from the girls' adolescence in part 1 to adult decisions and losses in part 2. to show how the novel develops the idea that growing up means carrying ideals into a more complicated adult world. A final section could consider jo leaves home for a new york boarding house, where she writes for money and meets friedrich bhaer. and judge how these details together support the claim that Alcott uses a temporary city setting to widen Jo's horizons without abandoning the novel's ethical focus. Reward close reference to scenes, accurate discussion of language and sustained evaluation.",
