            q: "Critical essay (12 marks): Assess the significance of Laurie in Alcott's representation of character and ideas.",
            sample: "Strong responses will argue that Laurie exposes the limits of conventional courtship expectations. A clear structure might begin with Laurie is the lonely boy next door whose friendship with Jo deepens into a proposal she refuses. It could then connect this to friedrich bhaer is the older german professor who challenges jo's sensational writing and later marries her. to show how the novel develops the idea that the right partnership supports growth rather than vanity. A final section could consider john brooke is laurie's modest tutor and meg's sincere husband. and judge how these details together support the claim that Brooke helps Alcott detach marriage from financial spectacle. Reward close reference to scenes, accurate discussion of language and sustained evaluation.",
